  "narrative": "During the 1930s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in tradition and identity. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth of kinky hair, became a symbol of pride and resistance against the societal norms that sought to straighten and conform their hair. Cornrows, a traditional style of braiding practiced in many African cultures, continued to be a staple in Black women's hair care routines, often done in simple or elaborate designs. Protective hairstyles, such as Bantu knots and box braids, were also popular, serving as a way to minimize manipulation and exposure to environmental elements that could damage Afro-textured hair. Although the source material does not provide specific information on the 1930s, it is clear that these styles were already an integral part of Black and African women's hair culture, paving the way for future generations to celebrate their natural textures and identities.",
